---
import Learn from "../layouts/Learn.astro";
import * as CH from "./yoy-and-mom.charts.js";
const NUM = CH.NUM;
---
<Learn
  title="Year-on-year, month-on-month · Measure of India"
  description="Two ways to measure the same change. Year-on-year cancels the seasons and moves slowly; month-on-month is fast and mostly noise. When each is the right tool."
  slug="yoy-and-mom"
  kicker="Prices: Year-on-year, month-on-month"
  h1="One change, <i>two speeds.</i>"
  lede="Year-on-year cancels the seasons and moves slowly. Month-on-month is fast and mostly noise. When each is the right tool, and why three consecutive months is the minimum unit of meaning."
  meta={[
    ["Concept", "y/y and m/m"],
    ["Illustrated with", "Headline CPI, both measures, 2021–2025"],
    ["Source", "MoSPI, NSO"],
    ["Level", "First principles"],
    ["Reading time", "6 min"],
  ]}
  dateStamp="Evergreen"
  crumbTitle="y/y and m/m"
>

  <!-- § 01 the definitions -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>The word, defined twice</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">First principles</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>The same change can be measured against two different pasts. Compare a month with the same month a year ago and you get the year-on-year rate, the form in which inflation reaches the news. Compare it with the month just gone and you get the month-on-month rate, the form in which inflation reaches the trading desk. One measure is slow and clean. The other is fast and dirty.</p>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Year-on-year <span class="sub">y/y, the standard comparison</span></div>
          <p>The change against the same month a year earlier: December against December, not against November. Comparing like with like cancels the seasons, because both months contain the same festivals and the same harvests. What remains is slower, cleaner change.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> the measure the news means when it says the inflation rate.</div>
        </aside>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Month-on-month <span class="sub">m/m, the fast comparison</span></div>
          <p>The change against the previous month: December against November. It answers quickly, but it compares two different seasons, so in India, where prices keep a festival and harvest calendar, much of what it reports is the calendar itself.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> speed, paid for in noise.</div>
        </aside>
        <p style="margin-top:1.4em">The trade-off cannot be engineered away. To remove the seasons you must wait a year for a like-for-like comparison; to be fast you must accept the seasons as static. Statisticians in some countries adjust the monthly data for seasonality and get a cleaner fast measure. India's published CPI carries no such adjustment, so the raw monthly change is noisier here than in most rich countries.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, i</span>
          <b>Slow and clean, or fast and dirty.</b> Every choice of comparison window is a choice about which flaw you can live with.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, ii</span>
          <b>Three months is the unit of meaning.</b> One monthly print is weather. Three pointing the same way begin to be a climate.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, iii</span>
          <b>The news uses y/y for a reason.</b> It is the only comparison in which December is allowed to meet December.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 02 plate : the two measures -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>Five years, heard at two speeds</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">y/y %, m/m %</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-ym" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The signal and the noise: one index, two measures of change</h3>
            <div class="sub">CPI combined, year-on-year and month-on-month, per cent, Jan 2021 to Dec 2025, m/m computed from the published index</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-ym" set:html={NUM["pv-ym"]}></div><div class="c">Dec 2025, y/y</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-ym" set:html={CH.ym()} />
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, CPI combined index, base 2012=100, vintage <span id="pf-ym" set:html={NUM["pf-ym"]}></span>, m/m computed from published levels; not seasonally adjusted</span>
            <span class="dl">
              <button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The blue line is the year-on-year rate; the thin grey line is the month-on-month change, computed from the same index. Watch how differently they behave. The grey line changes sign <b id="ev-flips" set:html={NUM["ev-flips"]}></b> times in five years. The blue line never crosses zero once.</p>
            <p>That is the seasons leaving the grey line and cancelling from the blue one. Vegetables flood the winter market and the grey line dips; the summer heat lifts them and it jumps. The blue line compares each month with its twin a year back, so the calendar cancels and only real change remains.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">The July 2023 case</span>
            <p>In July 2023 a tomato shock hit: the grey line jumped <b id="ex-mjump" set:html={NUM["ex-mjump"]}></b> in a single month, the largest monthly move in the window. A reader of the fast measure knew within weeks that something had happened. A reader of the slow measure watched the year-on-year line climb to <b id="ex-yjul" set:html={NUM["ex-yjul"]}></b> and learned, a few months later, how large the something was.</p>
            <p>Neither reader was wrong. The fast measure caught the event; the slow measure sized it. The mistake is asking one line to do the other's job.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 03 the unit of meaning -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>The minimum unit of meaning</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">A working rule, three months</span></div>
    <div class="numbers">
      <div class="num"><div class="l">m/m, Dec 2025</div><div class="v" id="n-ym-mom" set:html={NUM["n-ym-mom"]}></div><div class="d">one ordinary month</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">y/y, Dec 2025</div><div class="v acc" id="n-ym-yoy" set:html={NUM["n-ym-yoy"]}></div><div class="d">the same month, measured slowly</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Largest monthly move, 2021–25</div><div class="v" id="n-ym-max" set:html={NUM["n-ym-max"]}></div><div class="d">Jul 2023, the tomato shock</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Sign changes in the m/m line</div><div class="v" id="n-ym-flips" set:html={NUM["n-ym-flips"]}></div><div class="d">of 59 month-to-month transitions</div></div>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:40px">So how much speed can you trust? A working rule: one monthly print is weather, and three consecutive prints pointing the same way are the beginning of a climate. The rule exists because of what the numbers block shows. In five years the fast line flips direction <b id="ar-flips" set:html={NUM["ar-flips"]}></b> times; treating any single flip as news would have meant crying turn, on average, once a quarter.</p>
    <p>Used together, the two measures are a sequence, not a contest. The monthly line is the smoke alarm: quick to sound, often wrong, worth hearing. The yearly line is the verdict: slow, calm, and the only number the RBI's target actually applies to.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- § 04 misreads -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">04</span><h2>How to misread this concept</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“One month tells a story.”</h3>
          <p>One month tells an event. The monthly line in this window dips below zero in <b id="mr1-neg" set:html={NUM["mr1-neg"]}></b> of sixty months, and in every one of those months the basket still cost more than a year earlier. A story needs three consecutive months, minimum.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“y/y hides what happened in a single month.”</h3>
          <p>It does, deliberately. The present month's move enters the year-on-year rate diluted by eleven others, which is the price of cancelling Diwali and the monsoon. When you genuinely need speed, the monthly line exists. Refuse to read it alone.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“m/m is the honest measure.”</h3>
          <p>It is the fast measure, which is not the same thing. An unadjusted monthly change compares two different seasons and reports the difference as news. Honesty has nothing to do with frequency. It is about knowing what your comparison actually contains, month after month.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div slot="prov-source">
    <h6>Source of record</h6>
    <p>Ministry of Statistics &amp; Programme Implementation, National Statistical Office. CPI combined index on base 2012=100; retrieved via the eSankhyiki API, vintage 2026-08-04. The year-on-year rate is computed from the published index; the month-on-month rate is computed from the same levels.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-revision">
    <h6>Definitions &amp; honesty</h6>
    <p>India publishes no seasonally adjusted CPI, so the month-on-month figures here are raw and contain the calendar. The three-month rule is a reading convention, not a statistical guarantee.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-note">
    <h6>This note</h6>
    <p>Prices, version 1.0. Evergreen, written to survive the next print. July 2023 and December 2025 are used as history, not news; the chart redraws from the baked data on every build.</p>
    <p>Companion guide: <a href="/guides/cpi/">Consumer Price Index</a>.</p>
  </div>

</Learn>
